From 8848922569111bd046f5865f4b1995aef27247d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Brue <ryanbrue.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 14:09:46 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: mediatek: add the Amazon Fire HD 10 (2017)

The Amazon Fire HD 10 (2017), "suez", is an MT8173 tablet with the MT6397
PMIC and MT6311 secondary buck, a 1200x1920 Novatek NT51021 DSI panel with
an LP8557 backlight, a FocalTech FT5726 touchscreen, a BQ24297 charger, a
BMC150 accelerometer, an LTR-559 light sensor, a Realtek RT5514 microphone
codec, an MT6630 Wi-Fi/Bluetooth combo on SDIO, eMMC, a microSD slot, and
a PowerVR GX6250 GPU.

Its firmware is LK with PSCI 0.2 and no ARM Trusted Firmware, which shapes
the board file in three places: the CPUs use the mediatek,mt8173-smp enable
method so the kernel powers their MTCMOS domains; suspend-to-RAM is driven
by the kernel through the SPM, described as a child of the SCPSYS node; and
the VPU and the four blocks that depend on it are disabled, because the
bootloader leaves the VPU's memory inside a region the kernel cannot map
and every suspend attempt otherwise aborts on it.

Every regulator constraint, interrupt, GPIO and pin setting is taken from
the vendor's board file for this hardware revision (suez_dvt.dts in the
Fire OS 3.18 tree) or from its driver custom data, and the battery node
carries the Desay pack's characterisation tables from the same tree, as
the unit's IDME identifies. Where the two sources disagree or a value was
established on hardware instead, the comment next to the property says so.

The DTB validates against the schemas. "make CHECK_DTBS=y" on this board
reports one thing, and it is not this board's doing: mt8173.dtsi's mmsys
node has had unschema'd assigned-clocks for as long as it has had them,
which a pristine mt8173-elm build reports identically.

The MT6630 WiFi/BT combo on SDIO is deliberately NOT described here. Its
wifi-pwrseq node needs compatible "mmc-pwrseq-mt6630", which has no binding
document and is implemented only by an out-of-tree bring-up driver, so
describing it in this file would add an undocumented compatible string to
mainline (checkpatch: UNDOCUMENTED_DT_STRING) for hardware mainline cannot
drive. mmc3, its pinctrl groups, its fixed supply and the mmc2 alias go
with it. They are carried downstream until that driver is rewritten as an
mt76 sub-driver and gets a real binding.

Signed-off-by: Ryan Brue <ryanbrue.dev@gmail.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/Makefile         |    1 +
 .../boot/dts/mediatek/mt8173-amazon-suez.dts  | 2651 +++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 2652 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8173-amazon-suez.dts

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/Makefile b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/Makefile
index a86fb313b..ec85d7c49 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/Makefile
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/Makefile
@@ -93,6 +93,7 @@ mt7988a-bananapi-bpi-r4-pro-8x-sd-cn18-dtbs := \
 	mt7988a-bananapi-bpi-r4-pro-cn18.dtbo
 dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_MEDIATEK) += mt7988a-bananapi-bpi-r4-pro-8x-sd-cn18.dtb
 dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_MEDIATEK) += mt8167-pumpkin.dtb
+dtb-$(CONFIG_ARCH_MEDIATEK) += mt8173-amazon-suez.dtb
