+/*
+ * The GPU power domain, restored. History, because it bit us twice:
+ *
+ * mtk-power-controller refuses to probe if power-domain@7 (mfg_async) has no
+ * supply, and without the power controller nothing gets power domains at all
+ * - so for most of the bringup this node was /delete-node/'d outright, after
+ * an early attempt at giving it the MT6397 vgpu buck hung the SoC at scpsys
+ * probe (log stopped right after the regulator link was created).
+ *
+ * What changed: Chen-Yu Tsai's mfgtop series (carried in our tree) exists
+ * precisely because mfg_async's clock in mt8173.dtsi was wrong - clk26m
+ * instead of CLK_TOP_AXI_MFG_IN_SEL - and a wrong domain clock during the
+ * scpsys attach/power-sync is a plausible mechanism for that old hang. With
+ * the series applied (clock fixed, gpu + mfgtop nodes in the dtsi), this is
+ * the same wiring mt8173-elm ships upstream (domain-supply = vgpu buck) and
+ * works there. The GPU itself is a PowerVR GX6250 (BVNC 4.40.2.51), driven by
+ * the powervr driver once the mfgtop clock/power glue has probed.
+ */
+&mfg_async {
+	domain-supply = <&mt6397_vgpu_reg>;
+};
+
+/*
+ * The video processor and everything downstream of it, switched off.
+ *
+ * mt8173.dtsi enables the VPU for every board, but it does not work here.
+ * mtk_vpu loads both firmware images into the TCMs, writes VPU_RESET, and the
+ * coprocessor never signals back, on every single boot:
+ *
+ *   mtk_vpu 10020000.vpu: 4GB mode 0
+ *   mtk_vpu 10020000.vpu: wait vpu initialization timeout!
+ *   mtk-mdp 14001000.rdma: vpu_load_firmware failed -62
+ *
+ * Two things that look like the cause are not. It is not the
+ * secos-reserved-memory / vpu-dma-mem@b7000000 overlap the kernel warns about
+ * at boot: mtk_vpu only calls of_reserved_mem_device_init() when enable_4GB is
+ * set, this board has ~1.9 GB and logs "4GB mode 0", so that region is never
+ * attached and the extend memories come from the ordinary DMA allocator. Nor
+ * is it a missing firmware blob -- there is no "Direct firmware load failed",
+ * and both load_requested_vpu() calls succeed. The real cause is unknown.
+ *
+ * The vendor kernel offers no working configuration to work backwards from:
+ * it drives this block as its "md32" DSP (suez_dvt.dts enables the md32 node
+ * at the same 0x10020000) with proprietary firmware and its own driver, and
+ * mtk_vpu's firmware has never run on it.
+ *
+ * Leaving it enabled is not merely untidy, it breaks suspend. mtk_vpu_suspend()
+ * waits for a processor that never ran, times out, and returns -EIO:
+ *
+ *   mtk_vpu 10020000.vpu: vpu idle timeout
+ *   platform_pm_suspend(): mtk_vpu_suspend+0x0/0x120 returns -5
+ *
+ * which aborts every suspend attempt on the board.
+ *
+ * The four consumers go with it. Each one resolves the mediatek,vpu phandle
+ * through vpu_get_plat_device(), which does of_find_device_by_node() and
+ * WARN_ON()s when that returns NULL -- and a disabled node has no platform
+ * device. Disabling the VPU alone would therefore replace one timeout with four
+ * backtraces, from drivers that already fail their probes today. Turning them
+ * off also releases the mtk-power-controller, which otherwise reports
+ * "sync_state() pending due to 16020000.vcodec" (and 18002000, and 19002000)
+ * forever and never gets to disable its unused domains.
+ */
+&vpu {
+	status = "disabled";
+};
+
+&vcodec_dec {
+	status = "disabled";
+};
+
+&vcodec_enc_avc {
+	status = "disabled";
+};
+
+&vcodec_enc_vp8 {
+	status = "disabled";
+};
+
+&mdp_rdma0 {
+	status = "disabled";
+};
